# /{{VERB_REFINE}} — Idea → agent-ready ({{ORG_NAME}})

Refine is the **first half** of the flow. A ticket leaves refine only when it is
*agent-ready*: a `{{BUILD_AGENT}}` graph-agent could pick up any of its tasks and
execute without guessing. The bar is set by the wiki's operating model — read it (via
`/{{PLUGIN_NAME}}:{{VERB_PRIME}}`) before refining.

> **Why this matters:** agents execute well-specified work, not half-baked ideas. If
> refinement is thin, execution produces ambiguous tasks and the harness escalates
> back to you. Refine well once; execute cleanly after.

**Refine runs in this main session, with the engineer.** It is a graph you walk
yourself, never a dispatched or background worker: a subagent cannot ask the human
anything, and two nodes here wait on a human's sign-off (ADR 0019, `launch:
main_thread`). A refine can span days and people, so its state lives in the tracker,
not in this session.

## Invocation

```
/{{PLUGIN_NAME}}:{{VERB_REFINE}} <ticket-key>
```

The same command starts a fresh refine or resumes a paused one.

## Prerequisites

- `/{{PLUGIN_NAME}}:{{VERB_PRIME}} <ticket-key>` has run this session (you know the
  current flow + rules).
- The operating model's **refine** definition is loaded — it is the authority on
  what "agent-ready" means for {{ORG_NAME}} and may have changed since last release.

## The graph

| node | done when the ticket shows… | owner | next |
|---|---|---|---|
| `load` | the ticket, its comments, and the latest `REFINE-STATE` (if any) are read | — | `need`, or the node `REFINE-STATE` names |
| `need` | the user need, placed among existing needs | product | `validation` |
| `validation` | the validation/acceptance test, **signed by product** (gate: product) | product + engineering | `architecture`; `need` if the test exposes a wrong need |
| `architecture` | the repos touched, patterns, interfaces | engineering | `slos` |
| `slos` | user-facing SLOs with baselines | engineering | `cybersec` |
| `cybersec` | a security analysis of problem + solution | engineering | `trace` if a touched repo is regulated, else `deps` |
| `trace` | the design-controls traceability the work must produce | engineering | `deps` |
| `deps` | dependencies, deploy order, rollout, analytics | engineering | `sketch` |
| `sketch` | a repo × concern list | engineering | `ready` |
| `ready` | the agent-ready rubric passes (self-check) | — | `mark`; the deficient node (at most 3 times) |
| `mark` | the engineer approved and the `agent-ready` label is on (gate: engineer) | engineering | terminal |

## Walking the graph

- **Work conversationally, one decision at a time.** Pull context at T1 first; read
  the full ticket body only when a node's decision needs it.
- **Walk the nodes in order.** `validation` and `ready` are never skipped. `trace` is
  skipped only when no touched repo is under design controls, and you record that.
- **Write back at every node.** When a node is done, persist its artifact to the ticket
  (description section or comment), then post a `REFINE-STATE` comment before you move
  on. Post one at every pause too: a gate that is waiting, the engineer stopping, the
  session ending, an escalation. `<node>` is the node the next session resumes at.

```
REFINE-STATE <node>
settled: <one line per decided node, or a link to the comment that holds it>
next: <what <node> needs, and from whom>
validation: <signed by <name> (<comment link>) | pending | absent>
ready-loops: <n>
```

```
{{TRACKER_COMMENT_SNIPPET}}
```

- **The tracker is the system of record, not this session.** Keep the session lean;
  a fresh session must be able to resume from the ticket alone.
- **No PHI, PII or secrets in any comment.** Cite the system of record instead.

## The nodes

### `load`

Load the ticket at T1, then read its comments — prior refinement, sign-offs and
`REFINE-STATE` lines live there, and the view does not include them:

```
{{TRACKER_VIEW_ISSUE_SNIPPET}}
```

```
{{TRACKER_COMMENT_LIST_SNIPPET}}
```

Find the latest comment that starts with `REFINE-STATE`.

- **Found:** tell the engineer, in a few lines, what is settled and which node is next;
  on their confirmation go to that node. If it was parked at `validation`, check the
  comments for product's sign-off first. If it is `REFINE-STATE mark` and the label is
  on, the refine is done: report the `agent-ready` result line and stop.
- **None:** this is a fresh refine; go to `need`.

### `need`

Restate the problem as a user-facing need: `a <user> needs to <do X>`. Place it among
existing needs: search the tracker (and `design-controls/` in a regulated repo) for the
same or an overlapping need, and name the duplicate or the parent. The need is
product's (operating model); if the engineer is not product, say where product should
come in. An ambiguous need stops the walk (see Escalation).

### `validation` — gate: product

Co-draft the validation/acceptance test with the engineer: the concrete, observable
proof that the need is met, failing today. It is the outer red `{{VERB_EXECUTE}}`
drives to green. Add the acceptance criteria, each naming the test that proves it.
Write both to the ticket.

This is a human gate. It passes only when **product's sign-off is recorded as a tracker
comment** saying the test proves the need. If product signs in this conversation, post
a comment that names them and quotes their approval. Silence, or the engineer's belief
that product agrees, is not a sign-off.

- **Org-scope ticket with no product owner:** the owning engineer attests in place of
  product, and the comment names who signed and that they signed as the owning
  engineer. Who signs as "product" for Org scope is an open question in ADR 0019;
  treat this rule as provisional.
- **No sign-off yet:** write `REFINE-STATE validation` with `validation=pending` and
  end the run `parked:validation`. Do not continue to `architecture`: everything
  downstream is designed against this test.
- **The draft shows the need is wrong:** go back to `need`.

### `architecture`

In tech terms: which repos are touched (from the workspace's top-level `CLAUDE.md`
repo index; flag any new repo), which patterns, and how the pieces interface. An
architecture that implies an ADR-level decision stops the walk (see Escalation).

### `slos`

SLOs are always user-facing; only *who the user is* changes. Set latency, error-rate
and availability targets for the affected surface from the wiki's baselines (read them;
do not invent). Where the wiki has no baseline, follow the operating model's rule for
unknown thresholds (for example: propose one, mark it on the ticket as a guess, and get
the engineer's okay). If the operating model is silent, ask the engineer.

### `cybersec`

A security analysis of both the problem and the solution surface: the data handled
(PHI/PII), authn/authz, secrets, and any new exposure. A regulated surface whose
compliance obligation is unclear stops the walk (see Escalation).

### `trace` — regulated repos only

If a touched repo is under design controls (per the wiki's compliance rules, e.g. it has
a `design-controls/` directory), note
the NEED → REQ → SPEC → test traceability the work must produce. The wiki's
design-controls ADR is the authority on where those records live; the {{VERB_GATE}}
gate checks them. If no touched repo is regulated, record `trace: n/a` with the reason in
`REFINE-STATE` and go to `deps`.

### `deps`

- Cross-repo, cross-team and external-API dependencies, and the deploy order.
- Rollout: the feature-flag plan, canary criteria, rollback triggers.
- Analytics: the events that prove the journey and feed the SLO dashboards.

### `sketch`

Write a decomposition sketch to the ticket: one line per repo × concern, each naming the
acceptance criteria it covers and what it depends on. Every acceptance criterion is
covered by at least one line. The line count is `sketch=<n>` in the result line.

A sketch is not a task list: refine **does not create** child tasks.
`/{{PLUGIN_NAME}}:{{VERB_EXECUTE}}` turns the sketch into tracker tasks and validates them
before it dispatches anything.

### `ready`

A self-check, not a separate agent. Read this harness's agent-ready rubric from your
host's location for it: on Claude Code, `${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/rubrics/agent-ready.md`; on
opencode, `rubric/agent-ready.md` in the opencode config directory (beside `skill/`).
Apply every criterion to the ticket as the tracker records it. If you cannot read the rubric,
end the run `escalated:rubric-missing`; do not check from memory.

- **Pass:** go to `mark`.
- **Deficient:** show the engineer the deficiency list, go back to the earliest deficient
  node, and walk forward to `ready` again. Loop back **at most 3 times**, counting
  `ready-loops` in `REFINE-STATE` across sessions. If the check still fails after the
  third loop, end the run `escalated:ready-loop-cap`.

A ticket without a signed validation test is not agent-ready; that test is what
`{{VERB_EXECUTE}}` drives to green.

### `mark` — gate: engineer

Show the engineer the passing verdict and ask them to approve marking the ticket. Apply
the label only on their explicit approval. This is the only node that applies it.

The gate is a **board-agnostic label** (`agent-ready`), not a board-specific status, so
it works on any team's board without touching their workflow. Applying it *is* the
proof the ticket was refined and has its signed acceptance test.

```
{{TRACKER_GATE_SNIPPET}}
```

Then write `REFINE-STATE mark` with `next: /{{PLUGIN_NAME}}:{{VERB_EXECUTE}} <ticket-key>`.
`{{VERB_EXECUTE}}` refuses any ticket without the label. If the engineer declines, go to
the node they name, or park at `mark`.

## The result line

End every run — finished, parked or escalated — with exactly one line:

```
REFINE <key> <agent-ready|parked:<node>|escalated:<reason>> validation=<signed|pending|absent> deficiencies=<n> sketch=<n>
```

- `agent-ready`: the label was applied at `mark`. `parked:<node>`: the walk paused at
  `<node>` (a gate is waiting or the engineer stopped). `escalated:<reason>`: a stop
  condition below.
- `validation`: `signed` (a sign-off comment exists), `pending` (test drafted, not
  signed), `absent` (no test yet).
- `deficiencies`: failing criteria at the last `ready` check (`0` before `ready`).
- `sketch`: repo × concern lines written (`0` before `sketch`).

Every `parked:` or `escalated:` run has written its `REFINE-STATE` first. A human
resumes it with `/{{PLUGIN_NAME}}:{{VERB_REFINE}} <ticket-key>`; nothing resumes a
refine on its own.

## Escalation

Stop, write `REFINE-STATE` with the reason, and surface it to the engineer (do not
guess) when: the need is ambiguous, the architecture implies an ADR-level decision, a
regulated surface is touched and the obligation is unclear, the `ready` loop cap is
hit, or the rubric is missing. Humans are the top of the loop.

## Tool contract

Reads the wiki + tracker + codebase. Writes only to the **tracker**: refinement
artifacts, `REFINE-STATE` comments, and — at `mark` only — the `agent-ready` label.
Does not write to the wiki, write code, or create tasks.

